WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.600
Few people have heard of Walter Pitts&nbsp;
despite his revolutionary ideas that

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.680
helped set the stage for artificial intelligence.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.760
He was one of the first to show that&nbsp;
the brain functioned like a computer.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.240
But just when his career was taking off,&nbsp;
a series of tragic circumstances led him

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.880
to destroy his research, withdraw from&nbsp;
academia, and succumb to alcoholism.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:27.560
This is the story of the scientist who should have&nbsp;
been famous but instead died alone and unknown.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.720
You could say the odds of Walter Pitts&nbsp;
becoming a scientist were not in his favor.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:38.120
Born in Detroit on April 23, 1923, his father&nbsp;
and brothers were rough and uneducated.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.480
His dad actually wanted him&nbsp;
to quit school to get a job.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.520
But Walter was brilliant.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.600
As a child, he taught himself to&nbsp;
read Latin, Greek, and Sanskrit.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.960
He found solace in math, with logic&nbsp;
being the antidote to his chaotic life.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.720
The story goes that while hiding from&nbsp;
bullies one day at the age of 12,

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:01.360
he ran into the library and stumbled&nbsp;
upon a copy of Principia Mathematica,

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.520
a three-volume work that tried to show that&nbsp;
all math can be explained by using pure logic.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:09.240
He didn’t leave the library, even after closing.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:10.640
For the next three days,

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.840
Walter pored over the 2,000 pages&nbsp;
and sent a letter to its co-author,

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:19.120
Bertrand Russell, pointing out what&nbsp;
he considered mistakes in his work.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.280
Three years later, when he was 15,&nbsp;
and heard Bertrand was going to be

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:27.560
lecturing at the University of Chicago, he&nbsp;
ran away from home and went off to Illinois.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.960
He never spoke with his family again,

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.360
except for sending home an anonymous&nbsp;
Christmas present every year.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.720
For the next few years, he was unofficially&nbsp;
enrolled at the University of Chicago.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:43.760
While hanging around campus, a young medical&nbsp;
student he befriended, Jerome Lettvin introduced

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.480
him to Warren McCulloch, a professor of psychiatry&nbsp;
at the University of Illinois at Chicago.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.800
McCulloch wanted to learn how the brain worked,

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.640
delving into the process by which the&nbsp;
firing of neurons transforms into thoughts.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.880
Walter understood what was required,&nbsp;
and knew how to model it mathematically.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:04.800
McCulloch took him under his wing and into&nbsp;
his home, where they tackled this question.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.400
Their groundbreaking paper in 1943 envisioned&nbsp;
the brain as a collection of tiny switches,

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.640
each one turning on or off like a light&nbsp;
bulb, and these switches working together

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.880
to create thoughts and decisions.
 
McCulloch eagerly declared to a group

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:24.560
of philosophy students: “For the first time in&nbsp;
the history of science, we know how we know.”

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.280
It marked the first time anyone seriously&nbsp;
likened the brain to a computer.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.240
Their insight into how neurons operate paved&nbsp;
the way for the creation of neural networks,

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.520
now central to the machine learning revolution.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.640
Mathematician John von Neumann,&nbsp;
based his design on one of the

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.760
earliest electronic computers (EDVAC) on&nbsp;
Pitts and McCulloch’s model of the brain.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.000
Walter Pitts was a rising star.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.520
Fortune magazine included him in its list of the

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.960
top ten young scientists at&nbsp;
U.S. universities in 1954.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.480
Walter shied away from this&nbsp;
type of public attention,

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.160
seldom even signing his name to anything.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.880
Following his groundbreaking paper,&nbsp;
his friend Jerry Lettvin once again

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.240
played a crucial in introducing him to&nbsp;
another influential scientist, this time,

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.640
the renowned mathematician Norbert&nbsp;
Wiener at MIT who pioneered the field

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.280
of cybernetics…the study of how humans and&nbsp;
machines control and communicate information.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:21.560
Walter moved to Boston after working&nbsp;
on the Manhattan Project during the

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:25.880
war years to work for Wiener, whom&nbsp;
he regarded as a father figure.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:30.000
During their initial meeting, Wiener didn’t&nbsp;
say hello but instead, walked Walter over

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.560
to a blackboard where he was working out a&nbsp;
complex mathematical problem. Walter chimed

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.720
in with questions and suggestions, and&nbsp;
Wiener was impressed by the young man.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:44.160
He later exclaimed that Walter was: “...without&nbsp;
question the strongest young scientist whom I have

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.680
ever met … I should be extremely astonished if he&nbsp;
does not prove to be one of the two or three most

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:56.000
important scientists of his generation, not&nbsp;
merely in America but in the world at large.”

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.720
He had profound confidence in Walter’s abilities

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.480
and believed his neural networks could&nbsp;
be implemented into man-made machines.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.360
Although Walter’s work with McCulloch&nbsp;
was revolutionary, it was an overly

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:12.720
simplified representation of the brain; Wiener&nbsp;
wanted him to make his model more realistic.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.440
He promised Walter a Ph.D. despite the fact&nbsp;
that he hadn’t even graduated high school.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.040
Walter’s Ph.D. thesis focused on developing&nbsp;
an advanced version of his earlier brain

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.880
model using three-dimensional neural networks,&nbsp;
which was incredibly challenging and involved

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.760
some very complicated math, but those&nbsp;
who knew him were sure he’d succeed.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:33.840
But instead of Walter’s career taking off,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.720
it came crashing down when Wiener&nbsp;
suddenly cut ties with him.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:42.640
Because Walter was close to&nbsp;
McCulloch, whom Wiener hated.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:46.240
The rift began when the Director&nbsp;
of MIT’s Research Laboratory of

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:51.120
Electronics invited McCulloch to lead&nbsp;
a new brain research project at MIT.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.640
Walter, along with his friend Jerry Lettvin,&nbsp;
and a young neuroscientist named Patrick Wall,

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:58.840
were part of the team exploring&nbsp;
how the nervous system processes

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:02.400
information and how machines&nbsp;
might mimic these functions.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.200
Just as they were delving deeper into&nbsp;
the brain's workings, Wiener withdrew

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:10.800
his support from the group, reportedly&nbsp;
because his wife disapproved of McCulloch,

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.880
who had a penchant for alcohol and wild parties.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:18.160
Margaret Wiener is said to have invented&nbsp;
a story and told her husband that when

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:23.680
their daughter Barbara stayed at McCulloch’s&nbsp;
house, several of “his boys” had seduced her.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:29.080
It’s been suggested Margaret thought McCulloch’s&nbsp;
rise would threaten Wiener’s prominence at MIT.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:33.240
Wiener immediately sent an angry&nbsp;
telegram to the MIT director:

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:45.640
Wiener abandoned Walter, the young&nbsp;
man whom he had taken under his wing.

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:51.680
The final nail in the coffin for Walter Pitts came&nbsp;
during a heartbreaking experiment involving frogs.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.440
While it was believed that the eye took in&nbsp;
information and sent it to the brain for analysis,

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.880
Walter’s experiment found that&nbsp;
the frog’s eye itself processed

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:04.040
visual information like movement&nbsp;
before passing it on to the brain.

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:08.680
This contradicted Walter’s simpler, logical&nbsp;
model of the brain, in which he theorized

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:12.960
that the brain was the primary center&nbsp;
for processing all sensory information.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:16.200
Turns out, reality was much more complex.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:19.800
Lettvin recalled that the results&nbsp;
“...disappointed (Walter). He would

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:25.000
never admit it, but it seemed to add to his&nbsp;
despair at the loss of Wiener’s friendship.”

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.640
Walter destroyed his thesis, all of&nbsp;
his research, all of his life’s work.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:35.120
With the exception of writing one more paper, he&nbsp;
did little else but drink for the next ten years.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.320
Gone was his interest in science.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:40.320
Gone were his interests in&nbsp;
anything he had been curious about,

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:44.080
from poetry to geology to&nbsp;
the birds of New England.

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.600
In the 1950s, he moved out of&nbsp;
Jerry Lettvin’s home because

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.640
his heavy drinking scared his friend’s children.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.080
He spent the sixties in Boston’s bars,

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:56.520
changing his venue frequently so&nbsp;
that his friends couldn’t find him.

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:02.120
He was in such a bad state that he couldn’t&nbsp;
take care of his only companion, his dog.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:07.840
On May 14, 1969, Walter Pitts died&nbsp;
alone of alcohol-related causes in

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.840
a boarding house in Cambridge, Massachusetts.

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:13.680
He was just 46 years old.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.600
Pitts unknowingly set the stage for&nbsp;
modern computing and machine learning,

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:21.240
shaping a future that is&nbsp;
still evolving rapidly today.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.520
Sadly, we’ll never know how much&nbsp;
further progress might have been

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:29.040
achieved had his journey not&nbsp;
been prematurely cut short.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:32.640
Walter Pitts's innovations had&nbsp;
the power to change the world.

